      "stem": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Consider two factorial methods for n >= 0, ignoring arithmetic overflow:"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "text": "Method A:\nlong fact(int n)\n{\n  if (n == 0) return 1;\n  return n * fact(n - 1);\n}\nMethod B:\nlong factTail(int n, long acc)\n{\n  if (n == 0) return acc;\n  return factTail(n - 1, acc * n);\n}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Method B is initially called with acc = 1. Which statement correctly compares their recursion and\ncomputational efficiency?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "A is tail recursive because multiplication appears in the return statement; B is not",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "B takes Theta(log n) time because the accumulator avoids repeated multiplication",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "B always uses constant stack space in Java, while A uses Theta(n) stack space",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "B is tail recursive because no work remains after its recursive call; both take Theta(n) time, and B can use constant stack space if tail-call optimization is supported",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
